The code statements that lead to the bug are:
spin_lock_bh(&xtfs->x->lock);
hrtimer_cancel(&xtfs->iptfs_timer);
and
spin_lock_bh(&xtfs->drop_lock);
hrtimer_cancel(&xtfs->drop_timer);
Reason:
In `iptfs_destroy_state()`, each `hrtimer_cancel()` is called while holding a spinlock that the corresponding timer callback also acquires. For the output timer, the callback `iptfs_delay_timer()` takes `x->lock`; for the drop timer, `iptfs_drop_timer()` takes `drop_lock`. Because both timers use HRTIMER_MODE_REL_SOFT, their callbacks run in softirq context. If the callback is executing on another CPU, `hrtimer_cancel()` spins on `softirq_expiry_lock` (held by that softirq), while the callback waits for the spinlock held by `iptfs_destroy_state()` – creating an ABBA deadlock.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
hrtimer_cancel(&xtfs->iptfs_timer);
spin_lock_bh(&xtfs->x->lock);
and
hrtimer_cancel(&xtfs->drop_timer);
(with removal of the surrounding `spin_lock_bh`/`spin_unlock_bh`)
Reason:
The fix moves each `hrtimer_cancel()` outside the critical section protected by the respective spinlock. `hrtimer_cancel()` is safe to call without holding any lock and will wait for any in-progress callback to complete before returning. For the output timer, the lock is still acquired afterwards to drain the packet queue safely. For the drop timer, the lock/unlock pair is removed entirely because `hrtimer_cancel()` already guarantees serialization with the timer callback, making the lock unnecessary. This eliminates the circular dependency and resolves the deadlock.

